  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 505 AM EDT TUE OCT 13 2026\n\nThe extended portion of the forecast appears to be on track for a\ndistinct pattern change late in the week as a sharp autumn trough\ndigs through the Ohio Valley on Friday followed by northwest flow\nthrough the weekend. At the sfc, this will mean a well defined \ncold front passing through this part of the state on Friday \nsetting up chilly nights for the weekend when widespread frost or \neven patchy spots of freezing temperatures will be possible late \nSaturday night into Sunday morning. \n\nThe previous long term discussion follows:\n\nThe period begins Wednesday with eastern Kentucky situated between \nstrong yet low-amplitude ridging over the Southeast US and strong \nzonal flow to the immediate north, with a mid-level jet streak \nsituated across the Great Lakes region and a low/trough over \nHudson Bay. Upstream, an upper low is situated over the Four \nCorners Region and is ushering a warm moist air mass from the \nsubtropical East Pacific Ocean into the central US. Meanwhile, an \nupper trough is digging southeast through western Canada within an\nactive northern stream. \n\nModels are in generally good agreement on the overall evolution of \nthe upper pattern through the long-term period. The passing jet \nstream across the Great Lakes will push a cold front south to the \nvicinity of the Ohio River Wednesday into early Thursday, before \nstrong warm advection and the loss of northern-stream upper support \npushes the front back north as a warm front Thursday. Meanwhile, the \nwestern Canada trough will continue digging southeast into the \ncentral US mid-week while partially phasing with and absorbing much \nof the energy from the Four Corners low. This will strengthen the \nupper trough and sharpen a strong cold front that will barrel across \nthe Ohio Valley and eastern CONUS late Thursday through Friday. \nTransitory ridging moves across the region to end the weekend before \nthe remnants of the Four Corners low begins to move quickly toward \nthe Ohio Valley Monday as a weakening southern stream shortwave.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms chances increase Wednesday into \nThursday, especially north of the KY Highway 80 corridor, with \nstrong warm advection continuing and PWs much higher than normal. \nShower chances end Friday as cold advection upslope activity on \nthe back side of the cold front as the upper trough passes. Much \nabove normal temperatures with highs in the upper 70s to mid 80s \nare expected Wednesday and Thursday ahead of the front, with lows\nin the 60s.\n\nThis front will briefly usher in a much colder and drier air mass \nfor Friday into the weekend. In fact, it is possible that Friday's \nhighs occur in the morning, with temperatures remaining near steady \nor possibly falling through the afternoon as strong cold advection \noccurs. Saturday's highs will be the coolest of the Fall season thus \nfar, likely only reaching the 50s for most locations, with Saturday \nnight's lows possibly requiring frost and/or freeze hazard issuances \nas lows are forecast to drop into the 30s, with some potential for\na few locations to possibly reach near freezing. Warm advection \nbegins to increase again Sunday into Sunday night, with highs \nmoderating into the 60s and possibly lower 70s, with overnight \nlows Sunday night in the 30s to mid 40s.",
